<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Behbeit el-Hagar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Behbeit el-Hagar is an archaeological site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebennytos">Samannud</a>, the ancient Sebennytos. It holds the ruins of a great temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a> of the Thirtieth Dynasty and completed under the Ptolemies. Built almost entirely of granite, the temple later collapsed, probably in an earthquake, and its scattered carved blocks remain one of the main monuments of late pharaonic Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/behbeit-el-hagar.jpg" alt="Behbeit el-Hagar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Unger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BehbeitHigara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
